# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Prerequisites\">\n"
msgstr "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Prasyarat\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson builds on the stabilizer-check language introduced in [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i). It assumes you are comfortable with Pauli checks, syndromes, code distance, and the idea that surface-code checks are local on a 2D layout. A full formal treatment of stabilizer codes is available in John Watrous's [Foundations of quantum error correction](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/) course on IBM Quantum&reg; Learning, but the main text only requires the working ideas summarized in the optional refresher that follows. We stay at the level of *using* the formalism rather than deriving it.\n"
msgstr "Pelajaran ini membina atas bahasa semakan penstabil yang diperkenalkan dalam [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i). Ia mengandaikan anda selesa dengan semakan Pauli, sindrom, jarak kod, dan idea bahawa semakan kod permukaan bersifat setempat pada susun atur 2D. Penghuraian formal lengkap tentang kod penstabil terdapat dalam kursus [Foundations of quantum error correction](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/) oleh John Watrous di IBM Quantum&reg; Learning, tetapi teks utama hanya memerlukan idea asas yang diringkaskan dalam ulang kaji pilihan yang berikut. Kita kekal pada tahap *menggunakan* formalisme tersebut dan bukannya menerbitkannya.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"New to stabilizers, or want a quick review? Open this five-minute refresher.\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Baharu dengan penstabil, atau mahu ulang kaji pantas? Buka ulang kaji lima minit ini.\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This refresher is *optional* and only reviews the minimum notation needed for correction and decoding. It is enough to follow Parts 1-6, although the more detailed sections and [Section 3.1](#bb-construction) assume more.\n"
msgstr "Ulang kaji ini *pilihan* dan hanya menyemak tatatanda minimum yang diperlukan untuk pembetulan dan penyahkodan. Ia mencukupi untuk mengikuti Bahagian 1-6, walaupun bahagian yang lebih terperinci dan [Bahagian 3.1](#bb-construction) mengandaikan lebih banyak lagi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Pauli operators.** $X$ flips $|0\\rangle \\leftrightarrow |1\\rangle$ (bit-flip), $Z$ flips the sign of $|1\\rangle$ (phase flip), and $Y = iXZ$.\n"
msgstr "**Operator Pauli.** $X$ membalikkan $|0\\rangle \\leftrightarrow |1\\rangle$ (balik-bit), $Z$ membalikkan tanda $|1\\rangle$ (balik-fasa), dan $Y = iXZ$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Stabilizer/check.** A multi-qubit Pauli (for example, $Z_1 Z_0$) that encoded states leave unchanged (eigenvalue $+1$). Measuring it is a *parity check*, which returns $+1$ if undisturbed and $-1$ if an error anticommuted with it, *without* measuring the encoded data itself.\n"
msgstr "**Penstabil/semakan.** Pauli berbilang qubit (contohnya, $Z_1 Z_0$) yang dibiarkan tidak berubah oleh keadaan terkod (nilai eigen $+1$). Mengukurnya ialah *semakan pariti*, yang mengembalikan $+1$ jika tidak terganggu dan $-1$ jika ralat bersifat antiulang-alik dengannya, *tanpa* mengukur data terkod itu sendiri.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Syndrome.** The list of $\\pm 1$ check outcomes. It is a classical bitstring carrying *partial* information about the error (not its exact location, and nothing about the encoded state). A decoder infers the most likely error from it.\n"
msgstr "**Sindrom.** Senarai hasil semakan $\\pm 1$. Ia ialah rentetan bit klasik yang membawa maklumat *separa* tentang ralat (bukan lokasi tepatnya, dan tiada apa-apa tentang keadaan terkod). Penyahkod menyimpulkan ralat yang paling berkemungkinan daripadanya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Weight.** The number of qubits an error (a multi-qubit Pauli) acts on nontrivially. For example, $Z_4X_1$ has weight 2.\n"
msgstr "**Pemberat.** Bilangan qubit yang dikenakan sesuatu ralat (Pauli berbilang qubit) secara tidak remeh. Contohnya, $Z_4X_1$ mempunyai pemberat 2.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Code distance $d$.** The weight of the smallest error that can slip past all checks as a *logical* error. Larger $d$ means more protection.\n"
msgstr "**Jarak kod $d$.** Pemberat ralat terkecil yang boleh terlepas daripada semua semakan sebagai ralat *logik*. $d$ yang lebih besar bermaksud perlindungan yang lebih banyak.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**$[[n, k, d]]$.** $n$ physical qubits protect $k$ logical qubits with distance $d$. For example, $[[7,1,3]]$ protects one logical qubit in seven physical qubits with distance 3.\n"
msgstr "**$[[n, k, d]]$.** $n$ qubit fizikal melindungi $k$ qubit logik dengan jarak $d$. Contohnya, $[[7,1,3]]$ melindungi satu qubit logik dalam tujuh qubit fizikal dengan jarak 3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**CSS code.** A Calderbank-Shor-Steane (CSS) code has checks that split into pure-$X$ and pure-$Z$ types, which are handled independently. The surface code and bivariate-bicycle (BB) codes are CSS codes.\n"
msgstr "**Kod CSS.** Kod Calderbank-Shor-Steane (CSS) mempunyai semakan yang terbahagi kepada jenis $X$ tulen dan $Z$ tulen, yang dikendalikan secara berasingan. Kod permukaan dan kod bivariate-bicycle (BB) ialah kod CSS.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson asks not *what* quantum error correction (QEC) is, but *when it is the right tool for quantum advantage*. To answer that, we track where the cost comes from (Parts 1 and 2), how it can be reduced (Part 3), and where it *moves* rather than vanishes (Parts 4 and 5). Then we fold all of it into the advantage judgment (Part 6).\n"
msgstr "Pelajaran ini tidak bertanya *apakah* pembetulan ralat kuantum (QEC), tetapi *bilakah ia alat yang tepat untuk kelebihan kuantum*. Untuk menjawabnya, kita menjejaki dari mana kos datang (Bahagian 1 dan 2), bagaimana ia boleh dikurangkan (Bahagian 3), dan di mana ia *berpindah* dan bukannya lenyap (Bahagian 4 dan 5). Kemudian kita menggabungkan semuanya ke dalam penilaian kelebihan (Bahagian 6).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The earlier lessons introduced several ways to keep quantum computations accurate. We saw how physical errors arise and accumulate, how they can sometimes be suppressed or reshaped, and how their effect can be mitigated through additional runs and classical post-processing. The previous lesson added another idea: build checks into the computation so that a corrupted run can be identified and discarded. Its code-based syndrome post-selection, including spacetime checks, is a form of post-selected quantum error correction ([Qiskit Paulice blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qiskit-paulice)). This improves the quality of the retained data, but it also leaves limitations. Some errors pass undetected, a triggered check might reveal that something went wrong without identifying the most likely fault, and the method is most effective for circuits whose structure supports low-overhead checks. Decoding-based correction builds on the same check information. Instead of asking whether a run should be kept or discarded, it asks which error is most consistent with the observed pattern and how the encoded information should be recovered. The distinction here is between syndrome-based post-selection and decoding with recovery, two approaches along the same QEC continuum ([QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)). That extra power changes the resource trade-off. Decoding-based correction shifts more of the cost toward physical qubits, syndrome extraction, decoding, connectivity, and architecture. This trades sampling cost for additional qubits and hardware resources along the QEC continuum ([QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)), becoming central when lighter-weight methods cannot support the depth or reliability a task requires.\n"
msgstr "Pelajaran terdahulu memperkenalkan beberapa cara untuk memastikan pengiraan kuantum tepat. Kita telah melihat bagaimana ralat fizikal timbul dan terkumpul, bagaimana ia kadangkala dapat ditindas atau dibentuk semula, dan bagaimana kesannya dapat dikurangkan melalui larian tambahan dan pasca-pemprosesan klasik. Pelajaran sebelumnya menambah satu idea lagi: bina semakan ke dalam pengiraan supaya larian yang rosak dapat dikenal pasti dan dibuang. Pasca-pemilihan sindrom berasaskan kod, termasuk semakan ruang-masa, ialah satu bentuk pembetulan ralat kuantum pasca-pilih ([blog Qiskit Paulice](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qiskit-paulice)). Ini meningkatkan kualiti data yang dikekalkan, tetapi ia juga meninggalkan batasan. Sesetengah ralat lolos tanpa dikesan, semakan yang tercetus mungkin menunjukkan ada sesuatu yang salah tanpa mengenal pasti kesalahan yang paling berkemungkinan, dan kaedah ini paling berkesan untuk litar yang strukturnya menyokong semakan beroverhead rendah. Pembetulan berasaskan penyahkodan membina atas maklumat semakan yang sama. Daripada bertanya sama ada sesuatu larian patut dikekalkan atau dibuang, ia bertanya ralat mana yang paling konsisten dengan corak yang diperhatikan dan bagaimana maklumat terkod patut dipulihkan. Perbezaannya di sini ialah antara pasca-pemilihan berasaskan sindrom dan penyahkodan dengan pemulihan, dua pendekatan pada kontinum QEC yang sama ([blog kontinum QEC](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)). Keupayaan tambahan itu mengubah pertukaran sumber. Pembetulan berasaskan penyahkodan mengalihkan lebih banyak kos ke arah qubit fizikal, pengekstrakan sindrom, penyahkodan, kesambungan dan seni bina. Ini menukar kos pensampelan dengan qubit dan sumber perkakasan tambahan sepanjang kontinum QEC ([blog kontinum QEC](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)), dan menjadi pusat apabila kaedah yang lebih ringan tidak dapat menyokong kedalaman atau kebolehpercayaan yang diperlukan oleh sesuatu tugas.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson begins by connecting decoding and recovery to the post-selection methods of the previous lesson, then turns to the most established way to protect stored quantum information and why it is so expensive. From there it asks how that cost might be reduced and examines a concrete recent proposal as a case study. It then explains why protecting stored information is not enough for useful computation and what additional resources a full algorithm needs. Next, it steps back to view error correction as a whole system, including decoders, modular architecture, and the different challenges that other hardware platforms inherit. The lesson closes on the central question of [Lanes et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658), which asks when error correction helps preserve a quantum advantage and when its overhead erases it.\n"
msgstr "Pelajaran ini bermula dengan menghubungkan penyahkodan dan pemulihan dengan kaedah pasca-pemilihan dalam pelajaran sebelumnya, kemudian beralih kepada cara yang paling mantap untuk melindungi maklumat kuantum yang disimpan dan sebab ia begitu mahal. Dari sana ia bertanya bagaimana kos itu boleh dikurangkan dan meneliti satu cadangan terkini yang konkrit sebagai kajian kes. Kemudian ia menerangkan mengapa melindungi maklumat yang disimpan tidak mencukupi untuk pengiraan yang berguna dan apakah sumber tambahan yang diperlukan oleh algoritma penuh. Seterusnya, ia melangkah ke belakang untuk melihat pembetulan ralat sebagai keseluruhan sistem, termasuk penyahkod, seni bina modular, dan cabaran berbeza yang diwarisi oleh platform perkakasan lain. Pelajaran ini diakhiri dengan soalan utama [Lanes et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658), iaitu bilakah pembetulan ralat membantu mengekalkan kelebihan kuantum dan bilakah overheadnya menghapuskannya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"A note on scope\">\n"
msgstr "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Catatan tentang skop\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantum error correction is a problem for every hardware platform, but the form it takes differs. This lesson uses recent IBM&reg; work on bivariate-bicycle and gross-code architectures as a concrete case study, while keeping the main framing from [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658). Within that scope, we focus on *stabilizer codes on 2D superconducting transmons* (a *transmon* is the most common type of superconducting qubit), where the design challenge is to lay out checks on a flat grid and absorb non-local connectivity through modular hardware. Trapped-ion, neutral-atom, bosonic-mode, and topological-qubit platforms inherit different challenges, which we return to in Part 5. Until then, \"physical qubit\", \"stabilizer check\", and \"2D layout\" should be read as describing the superconducting setting.\n"
msgstr "Pembetulan ralat kuantum ialah masalah bagi setiap platform perkakasan, tetapi bentuknya berbeza. Pelajaran ini menggunakan kerja terkini IBM&reg; tentang seni bina bivariate-bicycle dan gross-code sebagai kajian kes konkrit, sambil mengekalkan rangka kerja utama daripada [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658). Dalam skop itu, kita menumpukan pada *kod penstabil pada transmon superkonduktor 2D* (*transmon* ialah jenis qubit superkonduktor yang paling biasa), di mana cabaran reka bentuknya ialah menyusun semakan pada grid rata dan menyerap kesambungan tidak setempat melalui perkakasan modular. Platform ion terperangkap, atom neutral, mod bosonik dan qubit topologi mewarisi cabaran yang berbeza, yang akan kita kembali kepadanya dalam Bahagian 5. Sehingga itu, \"qubit fizikal\", \"semakan penstabil\" dan \"susun atur 2D\" patut dibaca sebagai menggambarkan persekitaran superkonduktor.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We begin by importing the libraries used throughout the lesson.\n"
msgstr "Kita mulakan dengan mengimport pustaka yang digunakan sepanjang pelajaran.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 1: From detection to correction with stabilizer codes for error recovery {#part-1-from-detection-to-correction-with-stabilizer-codes-for-error-recovery}"
msgstr "Bahagian 1: Daripada pengesanan kepada pembetulan dengan kod penstabil untuk pemulihan ralat {#part-1-from-detection-to-correction-with-stabilizer-codes-for-error-recovery}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This part makes the relationship between detection and correction concrete. The check-based language and the notion of code distance are already familiar from the previous lesson, so we recall them briefly and look at how correction uses the same information more fully. This is the first entry in the lesson's running cost accounting. Correction buys more than detection, and the price appears first in the code distance it demands. Detecting a weight-$w$ error (one acting nontrivially on $w$ qubits) needs $d \\ge w+1$, while correcting it needs $d \\ge 2w+1$.\n"
"<span id=\"recap\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Bahagian ini menjadikan hubungan antara pengesanan dan pembetulan konkrit. Bahasa berasaskan semakan dan tanggapan jarak kod sudah biasa daripada pelajaran sebelumnya, jadi kita mengingatinya secara ringkas dan melihat bagaimana pembetulan menggunakan maklumat yang sama dengan lebih penuh. Ini ialah entri pertama dalam perakaunan kos berterusan pelajaran ini. Pembetulan membeli lebih daripada pengesanan, dan harganya muncul dahulu dalam jarak kod yang dituntutnya. Mengesan ralat berpemberat $w$ (yang bertindak secara tidak remeh pada $w$ qubit) memerlukan $d \\ge w+1$, manakala membetulkannya memerlukan $d \\ge 2w+1$.\n"
"<span id=\"recap\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.1 Recap and what changes for correction {#11-recap-and-what-changes-for-correction}"
msgstr "1.1 Ringkasan dan apa yang berubah untuk pembetulan {#11-recap-and-what-changes-for-correction}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The check-based language we use in this section is not new. [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i) already worked with stabilizer checks and their syndromes when introducing the $[[4,2,2]]$ detection code, and code distance appeared there as the smallest error weight a code fails to detect.\n"
msgstr "Bahasa berasaskan semakan yang kita gunakan dalam bahagian ini bukan perkara baharu. [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i) telah pun menggunakan semakan penstabil dan sindromnya semasa memperkenalkan kod pengesanan $[[4,2,2]]$, dan jarak kod muncul di sana sebagai pemberat ralat terkecil yang gagal dikesan oleh sesuatu kod.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"What changes in correction is how these objects are used. The same checks now return a *syndrome* that must do more than confirm something went wrong. It has to narrow the possibilities enough for a decoder to choose a likely recovery. The same code distance $d$ also plays a stronger role. A code of distance $d$ can correct any error of weight up to $\\lfloor (d-1)/2 \\rfloor$, which is roughly twice as demanding as detection alone (detecting a weight-$w$ error needs $d \\ge w+1$, while correcting it needs $d \\ge 2w+1$). These two upgrades, informative syndromes and a stricter use of distance, separate correction from the detection step of the previous lesson, and they set up the cost we examine in later sections.\n"
"<span id=\"repetition-code\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Apa yang berubah dalam pembetulan ialah cara objek-objek ini digunakan. Semakan yang sama kini mengembalikan *sindrom* yang mesti melakukan lebih daripada sekadar mengesahkan ada sesuatu yang salah. Ia mesti mengecilkan kemungkinan secukupnya supaya penyahkod dapat memilih pemulihan yang berkemungkinan. Jarak kod $d$ yang sama juga memainkan peranan yang lebih kuat. Kod berjarak $d$ boleh membetulkan sebarang ralat berpemberat sehingga $\\lfloor (d-1)/2 \\rfloor$, yang lebih kurang dua kali lebih menuntut berbanding pengesanan sahaja (mengesan ralat berpemberat $w$ memerlukan $d \\ge w+1$, manakala membetulkannya memerlukan $d \\ge 2w+1$). Dua peningkatan ini, sindrom yang bermaklumat dan penggunaan jarak yang lebih ketat, membezakan pembetulan daripada langkah pengesanan dalam pelajaran sebelumnya, dan ia menyediakan kos yang kita teliti dalam bahagian kemudian.\n"
"<span id=\"repetition-code\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.2 A worked example: the three-qubit repetition code {#12-a-worked-example-the-three-qubit-repetition-code}"
msgstr "1.2 Contoh penyelesaian: kod pengulangan tiga qubit {#12-a-worked-example-the-three-qubit-repetition-code}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The simplest example of correction-as-decoding is the three-qubit repetition code. One logical qubit, protected only against bit-flips, is encoded into three physical qubits, and a single bit-flip can be inferred from two parity checks, $Z_1 Z_0$ and $Z_2 Z_1$. The syndrome does more than signal that something went wrong. It indicates which qubit most likely flipped, so the logical value can be recovered by majority vote.\n"
msgstr "Contoh paling ringkas bagi pembetulan-sebagai-penyahkodan ialah kod pengulangan tiga qubit. Satu qubit logik, yang dilindungi hanya daripada balik-bit, dikodkan ke dalam tiga qubit fizikal, dan satu balik-bit boleh disimpulkan daripada dua semakan pariti, $Z_1 Z_0$ dan $Z_2 Z_1$. Sindrom melakukan lebih daripada sekadar memberi isyarat bahawa ada sesuatu yang salah. Ia menunjukkan qubit mana yang paling berkemungkinan terbalik, jadi nilai logik boleh dipulihkan melalui undi majoriti.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We use Watrous's [Foundations of quantum error correction](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/) course as the reference for the encoding circuit, stabilizer picture, syndrome table, and logical error curve, rather than re-deriving them in full here. As that course emphasizes, the repetition code is only a limited quantum code. It protects against bit-flips but not against phase errors, so it is best viewed as a pedagogical starting point only.\n"
msgstr "Kita menggunakan kursus [Foundations of quantum error correction](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/) oleh Watrous sebagai rujukan untuk litar pengekodan, gambaran penstabil, jadual sindrom dan lengkung ralat logik, dan bukannya menerbitkannya semula sepenuhnya di sini. Seperti yang ditekankan oleh kursus itu, kod pengulangan hanyalah kod kuantum yang terhad. Ia melindungi daripada balik-bit tetapi tidak daripada ralat fasa, jadi ia paling sesuai dilihat sebagai titik permulaan pedagogi sahaja.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two ideas from this example carry forward. First, the syndrome is *informative*, supporting decoding rather than just filtering. This is the step from detection to correction described in [Section 1.1](#recap). Second, the logical error rate improves only when the physical error rate is below a threshold-like crossover. For the three-bit repetition code, the decoded error rate $3p^2 - 2p^3$ is lower than the unencoded error rate $p$ only when $p < 1/2$. This simple picture foreshadows the more important threshold behavior that appears again in later sections, especially for the surface code in Part 2.\n"
msgstr "Dua idea daripada contoh ini diteruskan. Pertama, sindrom itu *bermaklumat*, menyokong penyahkodan dan bukan sekadar penapisan. Ini ialah langkah daripada pengesanan kepada pembetulan yang diterangkan dalam [Bahagian 1.1](#recap). Kedua, kadar ralat logik hanya bertambah baik apabila kadar ralat fizikal berada di bawah silang seakan-akan ambang. Bagi kod pengulangan tiga bit, kadar ralat yang dinyahkod $3p^2 - 2p^3$ lebih rendah daripada kadar ralat tanpa pengekodan $p$ hanya apabila $p < 1/2$. Gambaran ringkas ini membayangkan tingkah laku ambang yang lebih penting yang muncul semula dalam bahagian kemudian, terutamanya untuk kod permukaan dalam Bahagian 2.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Encoding circuit for the three-qubit repetition code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/repetition-encoder.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Encoding circuit for the three-qubit repetition code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/repetition-encoder.svg)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Encoding circuit for the three-qubit repetition code. Source: Watrous, [Foundations of quantum error correction, Repetition codes](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/repetition-codes), IBM Quantum Learning.*\n"
msgstr "*Litar pengekodan untuk kod pengulangan tiga qubit. Sumber: Watrous, [Foundations of quantum error correction, Repetition codes](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/repetition-codes), IBM Quantum Learning.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As the table shows, each single-qubit bit-flip produces a distinct syndrome, so the syndrome alone tells the decoder which qubit to flip back.\n"
msgstr "Seperti yang ditunjukkan oleh jadual, setiap balik-bit qubit tunggal menghasilkan sindrom yang berbeza, jadi sindrom sahaja memberitahu penyahkod qubit mana yang perlu dibalikkan semula.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/extracted-outputs/36475b64-0.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/extracted-outputs/36475b64-0.svg)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The encoded error rate of the three-bit repetition code is $3p^2 - 2p^3$. Majority vote fails when at least two of the three qubits flip, so the failure probability is $\\binom{3}{2}p^2(1-p) + \\binom{3}{3}p^3 = 3p^2 - 2p^3$. Plotting it against the unencoded rate $p$ makes the crossover at $p = 1/2$ explicit. The same visual logic (*encoded error below unencoded error below a crossover*) reappears for the surface code in Part 2, where the crossover becomes the code threshold and the physical error rate of interest sits far below it.\n"
msgstr "Kadar ralat terkod bagi kod pengulangan tiga bit ialah $3p^2 - 2p^3$. Undi majoriti gagal apabila sekurang-kurangnya dua daripada tiga qubit terbalik, jadi kebarangkalian kegagalan ialah $\\binom{3}{2}p^2(1-p) + \\binom{3}{3}p^3 = 3p^2 - 2p^3$. Memplotkannya terhadap kadar tanpa pengekodan $p$ menjadikan silang pada $p = 1/2$ jelas. Logik visual yang sama (*ralat terkod di bawah ralat tanpa pengekodan di bawah suatu silang*) muncul semula untuk kod permukaan dalam Bahagian 2, di mana silang itu menjadi ambang kod dan kadar ralat fizikal yang diminati berada jauh di bawahnya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This plot also explains when correction is worth its cost. For shallow circuits, the advantage is small, and the lighter-weight methods from earlier lessons (suppression, mitigation, and detection-and-discard) are often enough to keep results meaningful. The situation changes at depth. At larger circuit volumes, rigorous mitigation methods can require rapidly growing (often exponential) sampling overhead, and post-selected detection retains a shrinking fraction of runs. This applies to deep, general-purpose fault-tolerant algorithms, such as Shor's factoring algorithm, phase estimation, and the deep chemistry routines built on it. Correction becomes important there because it pays a large architectural overhead in physical qubits, decoding, and connectivity in exchange for an error rate that scales gently with depth. In the framework of [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658), near-term advantage claims might still rely on mitigation or post-selected detection where the depth and validation requirements allow. Decoding-based correction becomes central when those methods cannot provide rigorous error control at the required depth, while mitigation and post-selection can continue to complement it.\n"
msgstr "Plot ini juga menerangkan bila pembetulan berbaloi dengan kosnya. Untuk litar yang cetek, kelebihannya kecil, dan kaedah yang lebih ringan daripada pelajaran terdahulu (penindasan, mitigasi, dan kesan-dan-buang) selalunya mencukupi untuk memastikan hasil bermakna. Keadaan berubah pada kedalaman yang besar. Pada isi padu litar yang lebih besar, kaedah mitigasi yang ketat boleh memerlukan overhead pensampelan yang meningkat dengan pantas (selalunya eksponen), dan pengesanan pasca-pilih mengekalkan bahagian larian yang semakin mengecil. Ini terpakai kepada algoritma toleran-kesalahan serba guna yang dalam, seperti algoritma pemfaktoran Shor, anggaran fasa, dan rutin kimia dalam yang dibina di atasnya. Pembetulan menjadi penting di situ kerana ia membayar overhead seni bina yang besar dalam qubit fizikal, penyahkodan dan kesambungan sebagai pertukaran untuk kadar ralat yang meningkat perlahan dengan kedalaman. Dalam rangka kerja [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658), tuntutan kelebihan jangka dekat mungkin masih bergantung pada mitigasi atau pengesanan pasca-pilih di mana keperluan kedalaman dan pengesahan membenarkan. Pembetulan berasaskan penyahkodan menjadi pusat apabila kaedah tersebut tidak dapat menyediakan kawalan ralat yang ketat pada kedalaman yang diperlukan, manakala mitigasi dan pasca-pemilihan boleh terus melengkapinya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr "Semak kefahaman anda {#check-your-understanding}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Is a syndrome a quantum state or a classical bitstring, and what does it tell you?\n"
msgstr "Adakah sindrom keadaan kuantum atau rentetan bit klasik, dan apakah yang dimaklumkannya kepada anda?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Jawapan\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A syndrome is a classical bitstring, the list of $\\pm 1$ check outcomes. It carries partial information about the *error* (not about the encoded logical state, and without collapsing it). A decoder then infers the most likely error from it.\n"
msgstr "Sindrom ialah rentetan bit klasik, iaitu senarai hasil semakan $\\pm 1$. Ia membawa maklumat separa tentang *ralat* (bukan tentang keadaan logik terkod, dan tanpa meruntuhkannya). Penyahkod kemudian menyimpulkan ralat yang paling berkemungkinan daripadanya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Given a stabilizer code $[[n,k,d]]$, how do you tell whether it is used for error *detection* or error *correction*? Work out the detectable and correctable error weights for $[[4,2,2]]$, the Steane code $[[7,1,3]]$ (a seven-qubit CSS code holding one logical qubit at distance 3), and the gross code $[[144,12,12]]$.\n"
msgstr "Diberi kod penstabil $[[n,k,d]]$, bagaimana anda tahu sama ada ia digunakan untuk *pengesanan* ralat atau *pembetulan* ralat? Tentukan pemberat ralat yang boleh dikesan dan boleh dibetulkan bagi $[[4,2,2]]$, kod Steane $[[7,1,3]]$ (kod CSS tujuh qubit yang menyimpan satu qubit logik pada jarak 3), dan kod gross $[[144,12,12]]$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The same code can do either. Which one you get is a choice of how you process the syndrome, not a property built into the code. Detection works for any error of weight $\\le d-1$, and correction works for weight $\\le \\lfloor (d-1)/2 \\rfloor$ (from $d \\ge w+1$ to detect, $d \\ge 2w+1$ to correct a weight-$w$ error).\n"
msgstr "Kod yang sama boleh melakukan kedua-duanya. Yang mana satu anda peroleh ialah pilihan cara anda memproses sindrom, bukan sifat yang tertanam dalam kod. Pengesanan berfungsi untuk sebarang ralat berpemberat $\\le d-1$, dan pembetulan berfungsi untuk pemberat $\\le \\lfloor (d-1)/2 \\rfloor$ (daripada $d \\ge w+1$ untuk mengesan, $d \\ge 2w+1$ untuk membetulkan ralat berpemberat $w$).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| code | $d$ | detect up to | correct up to |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| $[[4,2,2]]$ (previous lesson) | 2 | 1 | 0 |\n"
"| Steane $[[7,1,3]]$ | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n"
"| gross $[[144,12,12]]$ | 12 | 11 | 5 |\n"
msgstr ""
"| kod | $d$ | kesan sehingga | betulkan sehingga |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| $[[4,2,2]]$ (pelajaran sebelumnya) | 2 | 1 | 0 |\n"
"| Steane $[[7,1,3]]$ | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n"
"| gross $[[144,12,12]]$ | 12 | 11 | 5 |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is why $[[4,2,2]]$ was detection-only in [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i). With $d=2$, the correction radius is 0. (Avoid two common errors. The *parity* of $d$ is irrelevant, and correction already works at $d=3$, not only at $d \\ge 4$.)\n"
msgstr "Inilah sebabnya $[[4,2,2]]$ hanya untuk pengesanan dalam [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i). Dengan $d=2$, jejari pembetulan ialah 0. (Elakkan dua kesilapan biasa. *Pariti* $d$ tidak relevan, dan pembetulan sudah berfungsi pada $d=3$, bukan hanya pada $d \\ge 4$.)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The bounds here ($d \\ge w+1$ to detect, $d \\ge 2w+1$ to correct) are the code's **combinatorial distance**, assuming syndrome measurement is itself noiseless. In a real fault-tolerant setting, the syndrome-extraction circuit is also faulty, so recovery is governed not by $d$ alone but by the circuit-level distance and threshold under repeated noisy measurement. We use this realistic model starting in Part 3.\n"
msgstr "Batas di sini ($d \\ge w+1$ untuk mengesan, $d \\ge 2w+1$ untuk membetulkan) ialah **jarak kombinatorik** kod, dengan andaian pengukuran sindrom itu sendiri bebas hingar. Dalam persekitaran toleran-kesalahan sebenar, litar pengekstrakan sindrom juga mempunyai kesalahan, jadi pemulihan tidak ditentukan oleh $d$ sahaja tetapi oleh jarak aras litar dan ambang di bawah pengukuran berhingar yang berulang. Kita menggunakan model realistik ini bermula dalam Bahagian 3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 2: Two-dimensional stabilizer codes and the locality-rate-distance trade-off {#part-2-two-dimensional-stabilizer-codes-and-the-locality-rate-distance-trade-off}"
msgstr "Bahagian 2: Kod penstabil dua dimensi dan pertukaran kesetempatan-kadar-jarak {#part-2-two-dimensional-stabilizer-codes-and-the-locality-rate-distance-trade-off}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two choices frame the trade-off in this part. The surface code uses *local* checks, which simplifies hardware implementation, but the overhead grows with scale. Quantum low-density parity-check (qLDPC) codes use *sparse non-local* checks, which can shrink the overhead but make the hardware and decoder harder. The rest of this part is about *why* that trade-off exists. The surface code is among the most studied quantum error-correcting codes, and it is a natural starting point for any discussion of correction overhead. Two features make it the reference baseline in the superconducting setting. Its stabilizer checks act between nearest neighbors on a two-dimensional grid, matching the connectivity of leading hardware, and it has a comparatively high noise threshold, so a useful logical error rate can be reached at physical error rates that are realistic in practice. This part introduces the surface code at the level needed to discuss its cost, then asks where that cost comes from structurally, and finally surveys the broader landscape of codes that relax the strict 2D-locality assumption. The reason this cost is hard to avoid is structural. For any code whose checks act only between nearest neighbors on a 2D grid, the dimensionless ratio $kd^2/n$ is capped at a constant, called the Bravyi-Poulin-Terhal (BPT) ceiling. That ceiling holds the advantage back, as [Section 2.3](#bpt-bound) develops.\n"
msgstr "Dua pilihan membingkaikan pertukaran dalam bahagian ini. Kod permukaan menggunakan semakan *setempat*, yang memudahkan pelaksanaan perkakasan, tetapi overhead bertambah dengan skala. Kod semakan pariti berketumpatan rendah kuantum (qLDPC) menggunakan semakan *jarang tidak setempat*, yang boleh mengecilkan overhead tetapi menyukarkan perkakasan dan penyahkod. Baki bahagian ini mengenai *sebab* pertukaran itu wujud. Kod permukaan ialah antara kod pembetulan ralat kuantum yang paling banyak dikaji, dan ia titik permulaan semula jadi bagi sebarang perbincangan tentang overhead pembetulan. Dua ciri menjadikannya garis dasar rujukan dalam persekitaran superkonduktor. Semakan penstabilnya bertindak antara jiran terdekat pada grid dua dimensi, sepadan dengan kesambungan perkakasan terkemuka, dan ia mempunyai ambang hingar yang agak tinggi, jadi kadar ralat logik yang berguna boleh dicapai pada kadar ralat fizikal yang realistik dalam amalan. Bahagian ini memperkenalkan kod permukaan pada tahap yang diperlukan untuk membincangkan kosnya, kemudian bertanya dari mana kos itu datang secara struktur, dan akhirnya meninjau landskap kod yang lebih luas yang melonggarkan andaian kesetempatan 2D yang ketat. Sebab kos ini sukar dielakkan ialah struktur. Bagi sebarang kod yang semakannya hanya bertindak antara jiran terdekat pada grid 2D, nisbah tanpa dimensi $kd^2/n$ dihadkan pada pemalar, yang dipanggil siling Bravyi-Poulin-Terhal (BPT). Siling itu menghalang kelebihan, seperti yang dihuraikan dalam [Bahagian 2.3](#bpt-bound).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.1 Stabilizers on a 2D lattice {#21-stabilizers-on-a-2d-lattice}"
msgstr "2.1 Penstabil pada kekisi 2D {#21-stabilizers-on-a-2d-lattice}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The surface code composes stabilizer checks into a two-dimensional grid. Data qubits sit on the edges of a square lattice, with ancilla qubits on the faces and at the vertices. Each ancilla measures a *weight-4 stabilizer* (either an X-type around a vertex or a Z-type around a face) on the four data qubits adjacent to it, using only nearest-neighbor two-qubit gates. The full syndrome-extraction circuit fits on a flat layout, which aligns the surface code with planar superconducting hardware.\n"
msgstr "Kod permukaan menyusun semakan penstabil ke dalam grid dua dimensi. Qubit data terletak pada tepi kekisi segi empat sama, dengan qubit ancilla pada muka dan pada bucu. Setiap ancilla mengukur *penstabil berpemberat 4* (sama ada jenis X di sekeliling bucu atau jenis Z di sekeliling muka) pada empat qubit data yang bersebelahan dengannya, hanya menggunakan gate dua qubit jiran terdekat. Litar pengekstrakan sindrom penuh muat pada susun atur rata, yang menyelaraskan kod permukaan dengan perkakasan superkonduktor satah.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Weight-4 stabilizer generators of the toric/surface code: X-type around a vertex, Z-type around a face](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code-stabilizer-generators.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Weight-4 stabilizer generators of the toric/surface code: X-type around a vertex, Z-type around a face](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code-stabilizer-generators.svg)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Two stabilizer generator types of the toric/surface code, each of weight 4. Source: [Foundations of quantum error correction, Toric code](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code), IBM Quantum Learning.*\n"
msgstr "*Dua jenis penjana penstabil bagi kod toric/permukaan, masing-masing berpemberat 4. Sumber: [Foundations of quantum error correction, Toric code](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code), IBM Quantum Learning.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two further properties make the surface code the reference toward later code families. The first is its noise *threshold*. Under realistic circuit-level noise, encoding helps once physical error rates fall below roughly one percent ([Fowler et al., 2012](https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.0928)), a level often quoted near the one-percent scale, which is one reason the surface code has become the baseline for superconducting architectures. The second is the maturity of its *decoding*. Under standard Pauli-noise assumptions, inferring the most likely error from a syndrome is often formulated as a *minimum-weight matching* problem on syndrome endpoints on the lattice, a problem solved by efficient classical algorithms.\n"
msgstr "Dua sifat tambahan menjadikan kod permukaan rujukan ke arah keluarga kod yang kemudian. Yang pertama ialah *ambang* hingarnya. Di bawah hingar aras litar yang realistik, pengekodan membantu sebaik sahaja kadar ralat fizikal jatuh di bawah kira-kira satu peratus ([Fowler et al., 2012](https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.0928)), aras yang sering disebut hampir skala satu peratus, yang menjadi salah satu sebab kod permukaan menjadi garis dasar bagi seni bina superkonduktor. Yang kedua ialah kematangan *penyahkodannya*. Di bawah andaian hingar Pauli piawai, menyimpulkan ralat yang paling berkemungkinan daripada sindrom sering dirumuskan sebagai masalah *padanan pemberat minimum* pada titik hujung sindrom di atas kekisi, masalah yang diselesaikan oleh algoritma klasik yang cekap.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Minimum-weight matching corrections on the toric code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-correction.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Minimum-weight matching corrections on the toric code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-correction.svg)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*A pair of syndrome endpoints (gray tiles) connected by a shortest-path correction (yellow). Decoding the surface code reduces to minimum-weight matching of such endpoints. Source: same lesson.*\n"
msgstr "*Sepasang titik hujung sindrom (jubin kelabu) disambungkan oleh pembetulan laluan terpendek (kuning). Penyahkodan kod permukaan terturun kepada padanan pemberat minimum bagi titik hujung sedemikian. Sumber: pelajaran yang sama.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The figure adds the part the syndrome leaves out. Because only the endpoints are visible, the decoder cannot see the actual error chain (magenta). It can only choose a chain with the same endpoints, and minimum-weight matching picks the shortest one (yellow) in this example.\n"
msgstr "Rajah ini menambah bahagian yang ditinggalkan oleh sindrom. Oleh sebab hanya titik hujung kelihatan, penyahkod tidak dapat melihat rantaian ralat sebenar (magenta). Ia hanya boleh memilih rantaian dengan titik hujung yang sama, dan padanan pemberat minimum memilih yang terpendek (kuning) dalam contoh ini.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The lattice geometry also changes what a logical operator looks like. In the $[[4,2,2]]$ code from the previous lesson, logical operators were short Pauli strings of weight equal to the distance. Here they are *string operators*, which are chains of single-qubit Paulis along a path in the lattice. A short chain of errors creates a string with two endpoints, and the stabilizers at those endpoints fire. The syndrome reveals *where* the chain ends, even though the chain itself is not directly observed. Seeing only the endpoints, the decoder takes the shortest chain joining them (yellow), which is not necessarily the real one. That is fine. The actual errors and the correction together close a loop, and a loop that shrinks to a point is a product of stabilizers, so the qubit is recovered even when the guessed path is \"wrong\". A logical error, by contrast, is a string with no endpoints. In the toric code, it wraps around the torus, while in the open-boundary surface code we focus on, it stretches from one boundary of the patch to the other. The distance $d$ is the length of the shortest such nontrivial string, and the number of physical qubits in a distance-$d$ patch scales as $d^2$.\n"
"<span id=\"surface-overhead\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Geometri kekisi juga mengubah rupa operator logik. Dalam kod $[[4,2,2]]$ daripada pelajaran sebelumnya, operator logik ialah rentetan Pauli pendek berpemberat sama dengan jarak. Di sini ia ialah *operator rentetan*, iaitu rantaian Pauli qubit tunggal di sepanjang laluan dalam kekisi. Rantaian ralat yang pendek mencipta rentetan dengan dua titik hujung, dan penstabil pada titik hujung itu menyala. Sindrom mendedahkan *di mana* rantaian itu berakhir, walaupun rantaian itu sendiri tidak diperhatikan secara langsung. Dengan hanya melihat titik hujung, penyahkod mengambil rantaian terpendek yang menyambungkannya (kuning), yang belum tentu rantaian sebenar. Itu tidak mengapa. Ralat sebenar dan pembetulan bersama-sama menutup satu gelung, dan gelung yang mengecut kepada satu titik ialah hasil darab penstabil, jadi qubit dipulihkan walaupun laluan yang diteka \"salah\". Ralat logik pula ialah rentetan tanpa titik hujung. Dalam kod toric, ia membelit mengelilingi torus, manakala dalam kod permukaan sempadan terbuka yang kita fokuskan, ia terentang dari satu sempadan tampalan ke sempadan yang satu lagi. Jarak $d$ ialah panjang rentetan tak remeh terpendek sedemikian, dan bilangan qubit fizikal dalam tampalan berjarak $d$ berskala sebagai $d^2$.\n"
"<span id=\"surface-overhead\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.2 Why 2D locality creates overhead {#22-why-2d-locality-creates-overhead}"
msgstr "2.2 Mengapa kesetempatan 2D mencipta overhead {#22-why-2d-locality-creates-overhead}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Being below threshold is a necessary condition, not a sufficient one. To reach a *target* logical error rate (say, $10^{-12}$ per round, a demanding stress-test target for deep fault-tolerant algorithms), the code distance has to grow until the residual logical-error probability drops to that level. The standard surface-code scaling has the per-round logical error behaving as:\n"
msgstr "Berada di bawah ambang ialah syarat perlu, bukan syarat cukup. Untuk mencapai kadar ralat logik *sasaran* (katakan, $10^{-12}$ setiap pusingan, sasaran ujian tekanan yang menuntut untuk algoritma toleran-kesalahan yang dalam), jarak kod mesti bertambah sehingga kebarangkalian ralat logik baki jatuh ke aras itu. Penskalaan kod permukaan piawai menjadikan ralat logik setiap pusingan berkelakuan seperti:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\varepsilon_L(d, p) \\;\\approx\\; A\\left(\\frac{p}{p_{\\text{th}}}\\right)^{(d+1)/2},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\varepsilon_L(d, p) \\;\\approx\\; A\\left(\\frac{p}{p_{\\text{th}}}\\right)^{(d+1)/2},\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "with threshold $p_{\\text{th}} \\sim 10^{-2}$ and a code-dependent prefactor $A$ of order $0.1$. The exponent $(d+1)/2$ says that each two extra units of distance gain roughly a factor $p / p_{\\text{th}}$ in logical error, so well below threshold, and the suppression is exponential in $d$, but only in $d$, not in qubit count ([Fowler and Gidney, 2018](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.06709)).\n"
msgstr "dengan ambang $p_{\\text{th}} \\sim 10^{-2}$ dan faktor awalan bergantung kod $A$ bersaiz $0.1$. Eksponen $(d+1)/2$ menyatakan bahawa setiap dua unit jarak tambahan memperoleh kira-kira faktor $p / p_{\\text{th}}$ dalam ralat logik, jadi jauh di bawah ambang, dan penindasan itu eksponen dalam $d$, tetapi hanya dalam $d$, bukan dalam bilangan qubit ([Fowler and Gidney, 2018](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.06709)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The qubit count grows quadratically. A bare distance-$d$ rotated patch uses $d^2$ data qubits and $d^2-1$ syndrome qubits, totaling $2d^2-1$ physical qubits per logical qubit ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7)). Combining the two scalings, reaching a deep target error rate forces $d$ to grow logarithmically with the target while the physical-qubit footprint grows as the square of that. The plot below makes the trade-off explicit. At a physical error rate of $p = 10^{-3}$, a reasonable level for current devices, reaching an illustrative per-round target of $\\varepsilon_L = 10^{-12}$ for one memory patch requires distance $d \\approx 21$ and 881 physical qubits in this model. This is a per-patch memory figure, not an algorithm budget. A computation uses many patches over many rounds, plus space for routing and magic-state production, so it does not mean that 881 qubits can run $10^{12}$ logical operations. In a separate memory benchmark, [Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7) estimate nearly 3,000 physical qubits in total to protect 12 logical qubits with the surface code, compared with 288 for the gross code at $p = 10^{-3}$. That comparison uses the paper's simulated memory performance, not the $10^{-12}$ target used here. Stretching the target by another factor of $10^{-2}$ in logical error rate adds only a few more units of distance, but the qubit count climbs by another few hundred. This overhead motivates the search for codes outside the strict 2D-local family, which is the topic of Part 3.\n"
msgstr "Bilangan qubit meningkat secara kuadratik. Satu tampalan berputar jarak-$d$ yang asas menggunakan $d^2$ qubit data dan $d^2-1$ qubit sindrom, berjumlah $2d^2-1$ qubit fizikal bagi setiap qubit logik ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7)). Dengan menggabungkan kedua-dua penskalaan, pencapaian kadar ralat sasaran yang mendalam memaksa $d$ bertambah secara logaritma mengikut sasaran, manakala jejak qubit fizikal bertambah mengikut kuasa dua nilai itu. Plot di bawah menjelaskan pertukaran ini. Pada kadar ralat fizikal $p = 10^{-3}$, tahap yang munasabah untuk peranti semasa, pencapaian sasaran ilustrasi per pusingan $\\varepsilon_L = 10^{-12}$ bagi satu tampalan memori memerlukan jarak $d \\approx 21$ dan 881 qubit fizikal dalam model ini. Ini ialah angka memori per tampalan, bukan bajet algoritma. Satu pengiraan menggunakan banyak tampalan dalam banyak pusingan, serta ruang untuk penghalaan dan penghasilan keadaan ajaib, jadi ini tidak bermakna 881 qubit boleh menjalankan $10^{12}$ operasi logik. Dalam tanda aras memori yang berasingan, [Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7) menganggarkan hampir 3,000 qubit fizikal secara keseluruhan untuk melindungi 12 qubit logik dengan kod permukaan, berbanding 288 untuk kod gross pada $p = 10^{-3}$. Perbandingan itu menggunakan prestasi memori simulasi dalam makalah tersebut, bukan sasaran $10^{-12}$ yang digunakan di sini. Meregangkan sasaran dengan satu lagi faktor $10^{-2}$ dalam kadar ralat logik hanya menambah beberapa unit jarak, tetapi bilangan qubit naik lagi beberapa ratus. Overhed ini mendorong pencarian kod di luar keluarga 2D-setempat yang ketat, iaitu topik Bahagian 3.\n"

